---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import SiteHeader from '../components/SiteHeader.astro';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
---

<Layout
  title="Guide: how Gantt Charter works"
  description="Full documentation for the Gantt Charter web app: entering tasks, dependencies and milestones, importing CSV, Excel or YAML, choosing palettes and page setup, and exporting vector PDF, SVG or PNG."
>
  <SiteHeader current="guide" />
  <main class="doc">
    <h1>Guide</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Everything the app can do, in the order you will meet it. The short version: add tasks
      or import a file, watch the live print preview, export. Nothing is uploaded anywhere.
    </p>

    <h2>Quick start</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Open the <a href={`${base}/`}>app</a> and either click <strong>Load sample project</strong> or add your first task.</li>
      <li>Set the title, palette and page under Project, Appearance and Page.</li>
      <li>Click <strong>Export PDF</strong> (or SVG/PNG). The file matches the preview exactly.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      Your work autosaves to this browser's local storage as you type, so you can close the tab
      and pick up later on the same device. <strong>Clear project</strong> at the bottom of the
      panel removes everything.
    </p>

    <h2>Tasks</h2>
    <p>
      Each task has a name, a start date, a finish date (inclusive), and optional
      <strong>Resource</strong> (who does it) and <strong>Phase</strong> (what stage it belongs to)
      labels. Bars are coloured by resource or phase, whichever you pick under Appearance.
      A task whose start and finish are the same day renders as a small diamond so it stays visible.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Hover a task row to reveal reorder arrows and the delete button. Chart order is list order.</li>
      <li>If the finish date is before the start date, the row is flagged and the task is left out of the chart until fixed.</li>
      <li>New tasks start the day after the previous task finishes and inherit its resource and phase.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Recurring tasks (multiple date ranges)</h3>
    <p>
      To show the same piece of work happening more than once — annual reports, recurring
      workshops, training that repeats each year — add the task again with the same name and
      different dates. Tasks whose names match exactly share a single chart row, and each entry
      draws its own bar on that line.
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>Click <strong>Add task</strong>.</li>
      <li>Give it exactly the same name as the existing task (capitalisation matters; extra spaces around the name are ignored).</li>
      <li>Set the new start and finish dates.</li>
    </ol>
    <ul>
      <li>The row appears where the <em>first</em> entry sits in the task list; later entries only add bars.</li>
      <li>Each entry keeps its own resource and phase, so a recurring task may change colour
        between occurrences (useful for handovers). Give every entry the same resource for
        uniform bars.</li>
      <li>Dependencies attach to the specific entry you set them on, so arrows point at that
        occurrence's bar.</li>
      <li>The same rule applies to imports: repeat the task name on several CSV, Excel or YAML
        rows with different dates.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Dependencies</h3>
    <p>
      Use the <strong>+ depends on</strong> control under a task to point at the tasks or
      milestones (marked ◆) it follows. The chart draws a thin elbow arrow from the end of each
      predecessor into the start of the dependent task; for a milestone predecessor the arrow
      runs from the milestone's guide line into the bar. Dependencies are kept when you save to
      CSV or YAML (as a <code>Dependencies</code> column and a <code>dependencies:</code> list
      respectively).
    </p>

    <h2>Milestones</h2>
    <p>
      Milestones are single dates that matter: approvals, deadlines, submissions. They appear as
      diamonds above the chart with a dotted guide line through it, in the same plum colour the
      Python CLI uses. Labels stack onto as many rows as needed so close milestones never collide.
    </p>

    <h2>Importing data</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>Import CSV, Excel or YAML</strong> accepts three formats. Imports replace the current
      task list, so save your work first if you need it.
    </p>

    <h3>CSV and Excel</h3>
    <p>
      The first sheet of an Excel workbook is read the same way as a CSV. Download the
      <strong>CSV template</strong> from the Data section to see the expected shape:
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Column</th><th>Required</th><th>Notes</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>Task</code></td><td>yes</td><td>Also accepted: Name, Title</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>Start</code></td><td>yes</td><td>Dates in YYYY-MM-DD work best</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>Finish</code></td><td>no</td><td>Defaults to Start. Also: End, End date</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>Resource</code></td><td>no</td><td>Also: Assignee, Owner, Who</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>Phase</code></td><td>no</td><td>Also: Group, Category</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>Dependencies</code></td><td>no</td><td>Task names separated by semicolons</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>Type</code></td><td>no</td><td>Set to <code>milestone</code> to make a milestone row</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>Description</code></td><td>no</td><td>Kept with the data, not drawn on the chart</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>Header names are matched case-insensitively. Rows with problems are reported individually and skipped; everything else still imports.</p>

    <h3>YAML</h3>
    <p>
      The YAML format is the one the companion
      <a href="https://github.com/sami5001/gantt-charter" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Python CLI</a>
      reads, so files round-trip between the two tools:
    </p>
    <pre><code>project:
  title: "My Project"
config:
  palette: "professional"
tasks:
  - name: "Literature review"
    start: "2026-01-05"
    finish: "2026-02-13"
    resource: "Researcher"
    phase: "Preparation"
  - name: "Data collection"
    start: "2026-02-16"
    finish: "2026-04-10"
    dependencies: ["Literature review"]
milestones:
  - name: "Ethics approval"
    date: "2026-02-02"</code></pre>
    <p><strong>Save as CSV</strong> and <strong>Save as YAML</strong> in the Data section export your current project in either format.</p>

    <h2>Appearance</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Colour palette</strong>: twelve palettes drawn from the University of Oxford colour system, identical to the Python package.</li>
      <li><strong>Colour tasks by</strong>: resource or phase. The legend follows the same grouping.</li>
      <li><strong>Legend</strong>, <strong>Gridlines</strong> and the <strong>Today marker</strong> (a dashed line at the current date) can each be switched off.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Page setup</h2>
    <p>
      Charts are laid out for a real piece of paper: A4 or US Letter, landscape or portrait. The
      preview shows the page at true proportions, and the time axis adapts to your date range,
      with week numbers for short projects, then months, quarters and years as the range grows.
      If the page gets crowded, the app warns you and suggests a roomier setup.
    </p>

    <h2>Exporting</h2>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Format</th><th>Best for</th><th>Details</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>PDF</code></td><td>Print, theses, reports</td><td>True vector at exact paper size, fonts embedded. Always has a solid page background.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>SVG</code></td><td>Web, further editing</td><td>Standalone vector file with fonts embedded, so it renders identically anywhere.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>PNG</code></td><td>Slides, documents, chat</td><td>Raster at 2×, 3× or 4× resolution.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <h3>Chart style and transparency</h3>
    <p>
      The <strong>Chart style</strong> switch redraws the chart with light ink on a dark page or
      dark ink on a white page; pick the one that matches where the chart will live. The
      <strong>Transparent background</strong> toggle removes the page fill from SVG and PNG
      exports so the chart sits directly on whatever is behind it. Use light style with
      transparency for light decks, dark style for dark ones. PDFs keep a solid page, because a
      PDF is a page.
    </p>

    <h2>Privacy</h2>
    <p>
      The app is a static site. Charts are built and exported entirely in your browser; no task
      data, file or analytics event is sent to any server. The only thing stored is your project,
      in your own browser's local storage.
    </p>

    <h2>Something broken or missing?</h2>
    <p>
      Please <a href="https://github.com/sami5001/gantt-charter/issues" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">open an issue</a>
      or read <a href={`${base}/about/`}>how to contribute</a>.
    </p>
  </main>
</Layout>
